{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0a39dc1d486e68710169bb2d01fcec8191b726c40f35f21e44", "created": 1773874434, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML): Blasts, Bone Marrow, and Diagnostic/Treatment Pathways\",\"summary\":\"This community is a tightly connected medical/biological knowledge network centered on Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) and its defining pathophysiology: abnormal immature white blood cells (myeloblasts/blasts) accumulate in the bone marrow and blood, leading to crowding out of normal blood formation. The relationships emphasize bone marrow as both the disease origin site and the primary diagnostic substrate (via aspirate/biopsy and blast percentage criteria), with diagnostic exceptions/confirmation strengthened by genetic and chromosomal abnormalities. A smaller set of nodes covers management concepts, including systemic chemotherapy/targeted therapy and hematopoietic cell transplant, and a contextual note that traditional staging is not useful for AML compared to solid tumors such as lung cancer.\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"AML is the central organizing entity, defined by excess immature myeloid cells (blasts) that accumulate rather than mature\",\"explanation\":\"AML is described as a fast-growing leukemia that begins in blood-forming stem cells in the bone marrow and is characterized by abnormal changes that prevent very immature white blood cells (myeloid blasts/myeloblasts) from maturing properly. This failure of maturation leads to accumulation of blasts in the marrow and often spillover into blood. [Data: Entities (1701, 2932, 1912, 2325); Relationships (3203, 3202, 4082, 2783)]\\n\\nMultiple entities represent the same biological concept under different labels (e.g., “BLASTS (MYELOBLASTS),” “MYELOBLASTS (BLASTS),” and “MYELOBLASTS (MYELOID BLASTS)”), reinforcing that the blast population is the key mechanistic and diagnostic pivot for the entire community. [Data: Entities (1702, 1924, 1891, 2932); Relationships (5489, 2783)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Bone marrow is both the origin site and the primary diagnostic substrate for AML evaluation\",\"explanation\":\"The network consistently ties AML to the bone marrow: AML originates in bone marrow tissue where blood-forming stem cells reside, and the abnormal buildup of blasts in the marrow is described as a defining feature of the disease. [Data: Entities (1701); Relationships (2781, 3147, 2783)]\\n\\nBone marrow is also highlighted as central to diagnosis and assessment, with clinicians sampling marrow via aspirate and/or biopsy to evaluate blast percentage and confirm AML. This places bone marrow sampling at the core of the community’s diagnostic pathway. [Data: Entities (1701, 1725); Relationships (2781, 2821)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Diagnosis of AML is rule-based around blast percentage (often ≥20%), with genetic/chromosomal exceptions and support\",\"explanation\":\"Across the community, AML diagnosis is framed around quantifying blasts in blood and/or bone marrow, with a commonly referenced diagnostic threshold of approximately 20% or more blasts. This blast-percentage criterion is treated as the standard anchor for determining whether AML is present. [Data: Entities (1725, 1924); Relationships (5807, 2821)]\\n\\nThe network also emphasizes that AML can be diagnosed even when blast percentages are below the typical threshold if specific defining genetic or chromosomal abnormalities are present, and that such cytogenetic/molecular findings can strengthen or clarify the diagnosis. [Data: Entities (1701, 1725, 1924); Relationships (5807, 2821)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Blast accumulation causes functional failure by ‘crowding out’ normal blood production\",\"explanation\":\"A core disease mechanism in this community is that blasts accumulate in bone marrow (and often the blood), physically/functionally displacing healthy blood-forming processes. This “crowding out” reduces healthy red cells, white cells, and platelets and impairs normal marrow/blood function. [Data: Entities (1701, 1713, 1913); Relationships (2791, 3147)]\\n\\nThe concepts “BUILDUP OF BLASTS” and “CROWDING OUT” are positioned as consequences of AML’s abnormal changes in immature cells, showing a causal chain: abnormal changes → failed maturation → blast buildup → crowding out/hematopoietic impairment. [Data: Entities (1912, 1913, 1713, 2932); Relationships (3203, 3202, 2791, 3147)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Treatment concepts include systemic therapy and hematopoietic cell transplant, with some regimens and CNS-directed therapy noted\",\"explanation\":\"AML treatment is described as occurring in phases with the overarching goal of achieving remission, adjusted based on ongoing test results and AML subtype/genetic context. Systemic therapy options include chemotherapy and targeted therapy, and an example combination referenced is daunorubicin plus cytarabine as part of treatment regimens. [Data: Entities (1701)]\\n\\nHematopoietic cell transplant is explicitly listed as a treatment modality used in AML management, indicating that the community includes both pharmacologic approaches and definitive cellular therapy options. [Data: Entities (1919); Relationships (3229)]\\n\\nWhen AML involves the central nervous system, therapy may include chemotherapy delivered into CNS-associated fluid (e.g., cytarabine and/or methotrexate), indicating the community captures at least one special-case treatment pathway tied to disease site involvement. [Data: Entities (1701)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community stresses classification/monitoring utility of genetic and chromosomal abnormalities beyond diagnosis alone\",\"explanation\":\"In addition to aiding diagnosis (including exceptions to the blast threshold), genetic and chromosomal abnormalities are described as being used to classify AML subtype, guide therapy selection, and monitor treatment response and remission status. This frames molecular/cytogenetic data as a cross-cutting capability that connects diagnosis to longitudinal management decisions. [Data: Entities (1701, 1725, 1924); Relationships (2821, 5807)]\\n\\nThis also implies the network’s logic is not purely symptom- or morphology-based; it integrates laboratory-defined abnormalities as decision points for subtype definition and ongoing assessment. [Data: Entities (1701, 1725); Relationships (2821)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Traditional cancer staging is noted as not useful for AML, contrasting with solid tumors like lung cancer\",\"explanation\":\"The entity “TRADITIONAL STAGING” states that conventional staging is not useful for AML because AML arises in bone marrow across many bones and flows through blood, which differs from organ-based cancers where staging tracks local growth and spread. [Data: Entities (1748)]\\n\\nA relationship uses lung cancer as an example of an organ cancer where staging is commonly used based on spread, reinforcing the conceptual contrast between AML and solid tumor staging paradigms. [Data: Relationships (2886); Entities (1748)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Overall structure is a clinical knowledge cluster rather than an organizational or reputational network\",\"explanation\":\"All provided entities and relationships describe medical concepts (disease definition, cells, bone marrow, diagnostic thresholds, genetic/chromosomal criteria, and treatment modalities) rather than organizations, individuals, or operational capabilities. There is no data about legal compliance, cyber/technical capabilities, or public reputation; the impact is therefore rooted in clinical seriousness and complexity rather than intentional activity. [Data: Entities (1701, 1725, 1919, 1748); Relationships (2781, 2821, 3229, 5807)]\\n\\nThe densest connectivity centers on AML ↔ bone marrow ↔ blasts ↔ diagnosis, showing a coherent, internally consistent framework for how AML is defined, detected